ts are related to the  Bavarian dialects in southeast Germany  and the Swiss dialects are related to  the alemannic dialects in southwest  Germany maybe you saw my video about the  languages of Switzerland it's this one  right here and in that video I talked  about how the Swiss German dialects are  kind of incomprehensible to speakers of  standard German unless they also speak  an alemannic dialect from the border  area but of course that's talking about  the Swiss German dialect Swiss Germans  can speak standard German even if they  tend to use it less than people in  Germany of course they might pronounce  standard German with a different accent  but that's no big deal in Austria and  South Tyrol the situation is similar  there are dialects that might be hard to  understand for people from some other  german-speaking countries but of course  standard German is the bridge these  countries are all part of the German  sprach realm  which means the geographic area over  which that language is spoken and within  that sprach realm standard german is the  dock sprocket dr. Pratt that means the  umbrella language under which all of  those dialects can come together those  two German terms I just used sprach  realm and Doc sprockie are common  linguist  terms and I think that shows just how  much influence the German language has  over academic areas of research like  linguistics so what is the German  language like well it's closely related  to English which is obvious when we look  at the most basic sentences here's an  example I buy books often it's kalfa -  you show ich kaufe  - foucha here the word order is SVO like  in English except that the adverb comes  before the object instead of after it  here's another example with an important  twist I will buy the book today if  you've added as y2 Calvin is Veda just  oh boy - Calvin in this case we see one  of the most confusing things for  learners of German which is the  placement of the second verb at the end  of the se